in inventoryService Repository wew have following case

public class Inventory {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy= GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private long Id;
    private String skuCode;
    private Integer quantity;
}

and

insert into t_inventory(quantity, sku_code)
values (100, 'iphone_15'),
(100, 'pixel_8'),
(100, 'galaxy_24'),
(100, 'oneplus_12'); you can see colmen name is different from what I have difined as model etity but still everything is working fine why?
ChatGPT said:

Everything is working fine because Spring Data JPA uses property-based mapping by default.
Why Is It Working?

    Column names in the database (t_inventory.quantity, sku_code) do not exactly match the field names in your Inventory entity (quantity, skuCode).
    However, Spring Data JPA automatically converts camelCase field names to snake_case column names when generating SQL queries.
    By default, JPA assumes a field name as the column name unless explicitly specified using @Column(name = "column_name").

How JPA Handles This?

    skuCode (Java) → sku_code (SQL)
        JPA recognizes camelCase and maps it to snake_case in SQL queries.
        It works fine even though the names are different.
    quantity remains quantity, so there's no issue.


